A||N||9||xfilesgirl1||GOT ANOTHER QUESTION! HELP!||||1||
Z||000000||xfilesgirl1||09-29-2000||06:55 PM||||ok...I just kinda remember one thing that I forgot to ask. What is a 1985 Fiero like in the winter. I live in Manitoba, Canada and we do get a lot of snow. So....would I get stuck on a gravel road? Or any road? Is it as low to the ground as a firebird or camaro? well,<BR>thanks,<BR>you guys have been a big help. May be looking at one tomorrow. hopefully.<BR>thanks,<BR>Alisa||||reg||1
Z||000001||maninblack||09-29-2000||07:10 PM||||Hi there, I live in Vancouver BC and for my <BR>exp.dont drive the fiero in snow,especially in your area.I am pretty sure you will get stuck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||09-29-2000||07:13 PM||||The '84 I had plowed through many a snowbank in it's day. You just have to be careful about the front and back trying to switch sides on you. It's not bad, you just have to remember that it's a rear wheel drive car. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||09-29-2000||07:17 PM||||Yes, you will get stuck on dirt roads. I have that problem with my 86GT. It would not be advisable to drive a fiero in snow unless you would like to buy my 84 beater once I put a new body kit on it:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jalap1.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jalap3.jpg"> <P>Man, this thing will turn heads when I go flying across the beach with it next summer.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||09-29-2000||07:19 PM||||That's something I'm going to have to think about as well.. I live in southern Ontario, and last winter I managed to put my moms car into a couple of snowbanks, and once on the front lawn... I guess you can't turn very well at 40kph on snow..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>(that's simply due to my amazing driving skills rather than any fault of the car, my point is that if it's so easy to do that in a FWD Ford Taurus... it's probably easier in a RWD Fiero...)<P>I'd like to get an older Jeep or Chevy Blazer as a winter vehicle.. but since I can't afford that, looks like I'll be taking the bus for a few months.. lol.. oh well.. since I'm at college, there isn't really many places I have to go.. the mall is 5 mins away, I live in residence, and I have several friends that drive home each weekend. I'll manage.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||12
Z||000005||theogre||09-29-2000||08:29 PM||||A stock Fiero has the same ground clearance as any other car. Ground clearance is actually USDOT regulated so most every thing built in north america has the same minimum clearance. (You can go higher but there's a limit to that to.)<P>Fiero can get pretty squirly in bad weather when it wants to. You'll need to take it someplace really open and practice with it. The Fiero setup is diferant even from most RWD cars.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2xsess||09-29-2000||09:55 PM||||I have to agree about the 84, I had one in 95 and we had a blizzard in Pennsylvania, they had closed the roads for a few days and I couldn't wait to get out so as soon as they allowed us on the roads I was in my car. The only catch was that the road I lived on still had about 8-9" on it. I just threw it in 2nd and plowed away. I also drove my 86GT threw an ice storm that had coated the road, but I kept the right side tires on the shoulder.<P>I would suggest a different winter car though.<BR>Sorry for that trip down memory lane <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>That Jalapeno is pretty wicked looking<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||olympic||09-30-2000||02:25 AM||||  Put a set of Bridgestone Blizzak tires on for the winter and you should be ok as long as you don't "hang the car up" on a snow drift.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DjKid||09-30-2000||03:04 AM||||I dont care what ya'll say, I like that 4x4 lookin thing.. paint that thing Metallic Grape(which is now a color I love) and get rid of the graphx.. it'd rock. I'd drive it, anyways. back to the question of Fiero's and snow. Well, last January we got 17inches of snow here in NC.. which shocked the hella out of alot of ppl. including me.. first thing I did the next day was go out and dig my baby out. I drove it through the snow.. she handled rather well. only probs I had was a broken alt belt, but that was gonna happen anyways.. and I left my lights on .. ooops.. I drive my car(or did) 12months a year. She is my daily driver.. come rain, snow, or shine.. she's like the USPS.. only when she gets disgruntled, I dont get shot! *ducks from the flames*<P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe - <A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||contra||09-30-2000||09:58 AM||||i drove my 85GT in the snow last year<BR>had NO problems except once i went to do a turn on fresh snow at 40kph, and the wheels turns, but the car kept going and onto somebody's driveway. no big deal really, it was only once. another bad thing was i saw a yellow light when i was going 50kph in the city, and i wanted to stop, but then my fiero didn't, so i kinda slid through the intersection for a half a second before i realized i should just keep going and risk running the light instead of crashing into something  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>but other than that, with my automatic, i just drive in D2 slowly when it gets really bad, D1 slides alot.<BR>i dunno if i'm gonna drive my 87GT in the winter. i own a jeep wrangler, but i dont feel like paying for gas in that thing<BR>ouch.<BR>||||reg||1
